<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bosphorus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bosphorus is a narrow strait in northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> connecting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a> and separating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> stood on its European shore, controlling traffic between the two seas. This strategic position underlay the foundation and long survival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>'s capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bosphorus.jpg" alt="Bosphorus" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Interiot · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkish_Strait_disambig.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Bosporus is a strait in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, straddled by the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, which connects the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a> and forms one of the continental boundaries between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Between its limits it is 31 km long, with a minimum width of 700 m between Kandilli Point and Aşiyan, and its depth in midstream varies from 13 to 110 m. Together with the Dardanelles it forms the Turkish Straits, and it is the world's narrowest strait used for international navigation. The name comes from the Greek Bosporos, folk-etymologised as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> passage, after the myth of Io, who was turned into a cow and wandered until she crossed it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>As part of the only passage between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, the Bosporus has always been of commercial and military importance. In the 5th century BCE Athens depended on grain from the Black Sea ports and kept alliances with cities that controlled the straits, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. The Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> crossed the Bosporus on a bridge of connected boats while marching against the Scythian horsemen.</p><p>In preparation for the conquest of Constantinople the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> built the Anadoluhisarı in 1393 and the Rumelihisarı in 1451 on either side of the strait. The final 53-day campaign ended on 29 May 1453 and transferred control of the Bosporus to the Ottomans, who made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> their capital. Control of the straits was an objective in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1877–78 and in the Allied attack at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> in 1915. The Montreux Convention of 20 July 1936 treats the strait as an international shipping lane while allowing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> to restrict the naval traffic of states without a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Most of the shores are heavily settled, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> extends inland from both banks. They were once lined with small fishing villages, whose names often keep the suffix köy, village, as in Ortaköy and Arnavutköy. The strait has 620 waterfront houses, yalı, from the Ottoman period, and Ottoman palaces such as Dolmabahçe and Beylerbeyi stand on or near it. Three bridges, a highway tunnel and a railway tunnel link the two banks.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bosporus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Constantinople on 11 May 330 CE, on the site of the ancient Greek colony of Byzantion at the crossing of the <b>Bosphorus</b>, is the conventional founding moment of what later scholars called the Byzantine Empire, though its inhabitants called themselves Romans and recognised no break in Roman continuity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From 603 his armies overran Armenia, Mesopotamia, Syria and Palestine, conquered Egypt by around 621, and twice reached the <b>Bosphorus</b> opposite Constantinople, culminating in the siege of 626, so that the empire reached an extent that rivalled the Achaemenid empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>A Rus' fleet of about 200 vessels sailed into the <b>Bosphorus</b> and began pillaging the suburbs of Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-18">18 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Yamaks refusing the uniforms of the Nizamı Cedit killed Raif Mehmet, the minister of the <b>Bosphorus</b>, and marched on Constantinople under Kabakçı Mustafa.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-25">25 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
